#edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290547 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92fe000e-8b10-5f6c-a7a8-e9bb8ce2fa57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290547 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297751 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{27}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297751 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297751 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f0cc90b-e38c-506a-8a4c-6a2e8028c58f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186298650 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186297751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186298650 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186298650 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186298650 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186297751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186298650 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "01a3424d-fe0a-549a-8dea-0f4f6a36be2c" #edb/